--- `all`, `any` or `find` handed a predicate whose answer can fail (here the
--- comparison reads a call that can fail, so `positive` answers a bool that can
--- fail).
---   interpreted: prints `true`; and a predicate that DOES fail counts as not
---                passing, silently: all[[1, -2, 3], positive] is `false` (.ok
---                true), any[[-1], positive] is `false`, find[[-1, 2], positive]
---                is 2 — while `if positive[-1]` stops with "cannot branch on an
---                error value — negative" (errors have no truth value).
---   compiled:    does not build:
---                error[E0308]: mismatched types  --> src/main.rs:52:194
---                error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler
---                refused what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
---                (the emitter writes `.all(|_x| positive(_x.clone()))` over a
---                function answering CrowResult<bool>; any and find alike).
--- Expected: both modes alike — a predicate that fails makes the builtin's
--- answer that failure (as a call hands its argument's failure on), or the
--- predicate refused before the program runs; the compiled program builds.
--- Met in roman_numerals_encode: all[range[1, 4000], round_trips], where
--- round_trips compares from_roman[to_roman[n]] with n and to_roman answers an
--- error value below 1. Worked around there by guarding the failure first
--- (`if not numeral.ok` / `return false`), so the predicate answers a plain bool.
fn checked[n]
    if n lt 0
        return error['negative']
    return n

fn positive[n]
    return checked[n] gt 0

log[all[[1, 2, 3], positive]]
